                           OVERLOAD/OVERRIDE ABSTRACTIONS

OVERVIEW

Procedures and functions in conventional Pascal are fixed, that is, the types
of parameters, return results, and body of the code contained are fixed.
However, it is possible to have abstractions on this principle that modify this
relationship. The three such abstractions are overloads, overrides, and
templates. Overrides and overloads are described here.

FUNCTION

Overloads and overrides for functions or procedures mean that the body of the
function/procedure can change, or the parameters and the body. To indicate
a procedure or function that is maliable, there must be a "root" declaration
as follows:

function x(a, b, c..): n; override;

function x; overload;

An override function/procedure has a fixed parameter list, but takes its body
from another procedure/function called the "overrider". The overrider is a
perfectly normal function with the same name as the overriden
function/procedure.

An overload function/procedure has no parameter list and no body. Both of these
are provided by the overloader function/procedure.